Nick sez:
> Also, is consciousness absolute or relative?  Will a Jupiter-sized
> brain consider humans to be conscious?

I am an objectivist, and therefore see everything as being absolute, for the
most part. This does _not_ mean there are not different levels of it, it
just means that the existence of Jupiter-sized brains does not diminish in
the slightest our own intelligence and consciousness.

But, also, it is a matter of semantics and definitions. We attribute things
such as continuity and self-awareness to consciousness. If there are other
attributes we lack, but which the Jupiter sized brain has, then maybe it
exhibits a characteristic beyond consciousness, but it doesn't mean we're not
conscious. Kind of like, even though we may think animals (stress on _may_,
since I know opinions vary on this) are unconscious, we don't attempt to
claim they are unintelligent, or that they are not alive. So, just as we
look down upon the unconscious organisms, perhaps higher organisms would
look down upon us as _only_ being conscious. :-)

