I agree on the risk part, and also that it _will_ happen.

> In summary, a conscious machine would be much more productive than an
> unconscious one for general-purpose application, but would make a lousy
> slave.  Rather, it would have to be more like a child, employee, or
> colleague.

Again, I question the conclusion. What proof is there that "a conscious machine
would be much more productive than an unconscious one for general-purpose..."?
First, define general-purpose. Much as our computers today can do a wide
variety of tasks for us, why wouldn't future ones be experts at a great many
things? Today, humans generally specialize in certain areas. Even with
transhumanist movements, I am not convinced that this will change much. THere
are always those who will seek to know a great deal about a great many things,
but for the most part, we all have a need to be the best at something, or
to make a real contribution in at least one area. The trade-off is generally
time. Generalization takes time, while specialization yields results much
more quickly. _Anyway_, if this is the case, then we would need to rely on
other resources for consultation in all other areas. We do this today. For
sickness, we see a doctor. For legal issues, we see a lawyer. To start a
business we might consult financial experts, manufacturing experts, human
resources experts, and the list goes on. So, _why_ not have unconscious
computers which can _be_ all that? Adaptive systems which constantly conduct
research to keep up-to-date, and alter strategies to maximize the value of
such a system. Does it _need_ to be conscious? I don't think so.

