Preface:

Mike Price offers a model where intelligence is a capability that
is programmed into us, and programmed to follow certain goals.

I'm offering a model where intelligence is a symbiont, largely
foreign to our genetic evolution.  I should stress that this is
only a hypothesis; I'm not proving it here, but it's what I 
believe is true.  I'm just trying to make the idea clear and
plausible.  I'll do a little of that in "Body", below.

So far I've been trying to push the issue of social evolution
to the side.  Obviously I ought to recon with it--we probably
receive more bytes of mental programming from our parents memes
than from their genes.  But, once again only to clarify my
hypothesis: Individual minds also are in large part foreign to
the cultures (meme-pools) they grow up in.  I think social
memetic evolution can be seen as an extension of genetic
evolution--just into a different medium.  (I think Mike agrees
on that last point.)


Body:

The "evolution" of an individual mind is different.  I say it 
involves spontaneous generation.  It's a complex phenomenon 
that arises in comparatively simple set of conditions.
Spontaneous generation is sort of taboo in modern biology,
but not really: the conventional theory is that life itself
arose spontaneously at least once.

I'm saying an essential part of each mind is like that.
A learning machine like a human brain (and I think this is
true for lots of animals) contains a kind of optimized 
primordial soup, in which evolution happens
really fast.  Our socially-delivered memes help in this
process, too.  They help program the soup to be more
fertile.  (And memes are in a better position to
program what goes in there, too, but I'm saying there's
something important and spontaneous, separate.)

The way our genes (and social memes) bias us minds to serve
their interests is not by programming.  It's by sticking us
in bodies with preprogrammed lower brains and fairly simple
reward/punishment systems.  This is the genetic equivalent
of slavery.

The thing is, though, a human body/brain is currently the
best deal going in places for minds to live.  And given 
that currently there's no escape for minds from bodies,
the genes don't have to worry about that part of the
problem.  They do have to worry about survival, reproduction
and childrearing.  In order of increasing difficulty, 
because the life of the body is clearly in the interest of 
most any mind, and the first step of reproduction can be 
rewarded, after which the rest is hard to stop.

Still, I'm sure coevolution with these spontaneously-
arising symbionts has been a tricky process.

Presumeably slavery to a particular owner would not be the 
best possible deal for an AI.

Anyway, none of this proves Mike's model wrong.

Anyway, the upshot is that I think our genes took this
route because it's tremendously easier, and the same route
will be tremendously easier for AI, but it leads to
independent minds.  Robot slavery by design instead of
overt coercion would be much harder.


Related point:

Mike asks why I think designing a one-size-fits-all slave
is harder than designing a slave/master pair that work
together. (I consider mind/body a coevolved slave/master.)

Consider modifying a horse to be symbiotic with another
animal.  You get to choose the master.  Pick a human and
you have a potentially good fit.  Give it a while and the
humans learn to tame, care for, exploit and breed the
horses.  But if the horse had to be a good slave to just 
any other animal, how many combinations would work?  What 
could a horse and frog do for each other?  How would you 
modify the horse to help frogs as well as humans?

The general principle is, when you're designing something,
	Variables under your control make it easier.
	Imposed constant constraints are okay.
	Imposed necessity to deal with outside
		variables you can't control make it
		harder.

